    pedagogy: 'Make certainty visible. Learners colour-code what the writer states directly, what is approximate and what only “some sources” report. The language work grows out of this: learners discover that “according to”, “around” and “some sources say” are the writer’s tools for honesty about evidence.',
    priorKnowledge: ['Because/because of; contrast with but; years and approximate numbers.'],
    anticipatedMisconceptions: ['Do not turn “some sources say” or approximate dates into certainty, and do not equate plain language with weak literary quality.'],
    grammarFocus: 'Targets: reason with due to + noun · balanced evaluation (neither too … nor too …) · combine A with B · help + person + base verb · still for a continuing influence · source stance (According to …, Some sources say that …, around + date) · but … also, which-clause and passive was taught.\nNotice (Activity 1): learners match five phrases from the first paragraph with plain-English meanings. Then ask: “Which phrase gives a reason? Which one tells us the influence has not stopped?”\nBuild (Activities 2–3): choose According to / around / say, then complete the madrasa–tekke lines with also, which, taught. After each item ask: “How sure is the writer here?”\nLikely errors: *According historical sources · *Some sources says · *was teach · who for a place.\nUse (Activity 4): a 5–6 sentence profile with one balanced evaluation, one approximate date and one source-qualified claim.',
    pronunciationFocus: 'Say source frames as single units: “acCORDing to hisTORical SOURces | …”, “some SOURces SAY that | …”.\nBalance with contrastive stress: “NEIther too SIMple | nor too COMplex”.\nDates: “around twelve forty to twelve forty-one”, “around thirteen twenty to thirteen twenty-one”.\nWord focus: MAD-ra-sa, PER-sian (/ʒ/), sig-NIF-i-cant, IN-flu-ence.',
    beforeReading: ['Read three statements aloud: one certain, one approximate, one reported (for example about your own school). Learners show 1, 2 or 3 fingers: certain / approximate / reported. Ask which words helped them decide.'],
    duringReading: ['Underline the reason his works reach many people.', 'Circle “around” in the dates.', 'Highlight “According to historical sources” and “Some sources say” in a second colour.', 'Separate madrasa information from tekke learning.'],
    afterReading: ['Complete the Quick Challenge while preserving source stance.', 'Language Focus: match the phrases, then report history carefully and connect the two kinds of learning.', 'Write a five-sentence profile with one balanced evaluation, one approximate date and one attributed claim.'],
    lessonPlan: '0–5 Certainty signals: three statements read aloud, learners show certain / approximate / reported with fingers and name the signal word; 5–14 Listen for gist (Why do people love his works?), then read and colour-code direct / approximate / reported information; 14–20 Pairs compare colours and justify each one with the signal word, then contrast madrasa and tekke in two lines; 20–24 Quick Challenge; 24–32 Language Focus: Activity 1 in pairs with a whole-class question (which phrase gives a reason?), Activities 2–3 individually with pair checking; 32–38 Use: a five-sentence profile of a writer or artist, partners check that no “around” or “some sources” has been deleted; 38–40 Exit ticket.',
    discussionPoints: ['Which statements are direct and which are source-qualified?', 'Why is plain language not the same as poor literary quality?', 'What changes when “some sources say” is removed?'],
    interactiveTips: ['Replay the source-attribution sentence before learners answer biography questions.', 'Use the Education hotspot to compare madrasa and tekke information.', 'Reject rewrites that silently remove “around” or “some sources”.'],
    differentiation: { strugglingLearners: 'Give frames: “Around ____, …”, “According to ____, …” and “Some sources say that ____.”', fastFinishers: 'Write a short biography of a real writer using four different stance devices and label how certain each sentence is.' },
